      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-222215",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $A \\cdot (B\u222aC)\u2286(A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot (B\u222aC)$.\n> $L2$: Then, $x \u2208 A$ and $y \u2208 B\u222aC$.\n> $L3$: In turn, $y \u2208 B$ or $y \u2208 C$, which gives two cases.\n> $L4$: *Case 1.* Let $y \u2208 B$.\n> $L5$: Then, $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot B$.\n> $L6$: *Case 2.* Let $y \u2208 C$.\n> $L7$: Then, $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot C$.\n> $L8$: Therefore, $(x, y) \u2208 (A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$.\n> $L9$: Thus, $A \\cdot (B\u222aC)\u2286(A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from {{field-1}} in line $L2$ and by the definition of {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from {{field-3}} in line $L2$, and line {{field-4}}, and by the definition of {{field-5}}.\n\nLine $L9$ follows from line $L1$ and $L8$ by the definition of {{field-6}}."
