      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-222344",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $(A\u2216B) \\cdot C\u2286(A \\cdot C)\u2216(B \\cdot C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $(x, y) \u2208 (A\u2216B) \\cdot C$.\n> $L2$: Then, $x \u2208 A\u2216B$ and $y \u2208 C$.\n> $L3$: In turn, $x \u2208 A$ and $x \u2209 B$,\n> $L4$: Hence, $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot C$ and $(x, y) \u2209 B \\cdot C$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $(x, y) \u2208 (A \\cdot C)\u2216(B \\cdot C)$.\n> $L6$: Thus, $(A\u2216B) \\cdot C\u2286(A \\cdot C)\u2216(B \\cdot C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nTo prove this subset relation, we must show that if $(x, y) \u2208$\n{{field-1}},\nthen $(x, y) \u2208$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nThe fact that $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot C$ in line $L4$ follows from {{field-3}} in line $L3$ and {{field-4}} in line $L2$, and by the definition of {{field-5}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line {{field-6}} and $L5$ by the definition of {{field-7}}."
